.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tam-bundle
packages a coherent set of files built from exact source commits, marks it
as one thing with a name, a version and a content hash, and installs it as
real files in real directories.
It records what it installed, switches between versions in one step, and
rolls back with one command.
.PP
It is not a general package manager.
Third-party software comes from the distribution's own packages; a bundle
only names the native commands and files it needs, and
.B tam-bundle
reports which are missing without installing them.
.PP
Nothing runs in the background: no daemon, socket, timer or polling.
.B tam-bundle
uses the network only when
.B build
has to fetch a locked commit that no local clone supplies.
.SS Words used here
.TP
.B bundle
A directory holding a built tree of files plus two records:
.B BUNDLE
(identity) and
.B MANIFEST
(file list).
It can be copied anywhere and checked with
.BR verify .
.TP
.B bundle id
.IB name \- version \- hash12\fR,
where
.I hash12
is the first 12 hexadecimal digits of the bundle hash, for example
.BR tamlinux\-0.7.0\-3f9a0c1b2d4e .
.TP
.B slot
The directory a bundle is installed into.
The default is
.BR /usr/lib/tamlinux .
Any directory works as a slot, including one in a home directory, which
needs no privilege.
.TP
.B live tree
.IB slot /current/\fR,
the files in use.
Its
.B BUNDLE
and
.B MANIFEST
say exactly which bundle is live.
.TP
.B kept tree
A previous live tree, kept whole for rollback at
.IB slot /kept/ bundle-id /\fR.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Private settings (0.7 development interface)
.TP
.BI capture " [\-o file] [\-\-save-edits]"
Capture the selected user's listed settings into private state below HOME.
Captures distinguish present, absent and excluded files; excluded files
are never restored or removed. Known secret paths and detected secret
patterns are excluded with their path and line number, never their value.
With \-o, export a mode-0600 workstation archive containing product and capture.
Edited product files require \-\-save-edits and produce a marked derived
bundle with its own identity. Workstation archives are private and cannot
be written under public product state.
Export publishes only a complete, synced archive and refuses to replace
an existing output. After interruption, verify a published output before
reusing it; otherwise repeat capture with the intended output name.
.TP
.BI import-settings " file"
Verify a transferred workstation archive and retain its capture privately.
This changes no home setting. The product can be prepared separately with
install; its system state never receives the private capture.
.TP
.BI prepare-settings " [capture-id]"
Record the user's plan for a ready system transaction. With a capture id,
plan restoration; otherwise plan an upgrade using old and new defaults.
Actual home files are read and automatically captured when apply-settings
runs, so edits after prepare are preserved.
.TP
.BI restore-settings " capture-id"
Plan restoration of settings onto the current product. Settings and
software from different products produce a warning. Common settings apply
on another host; host settings stay local, with the new product default
used only where the target has no file.
.TP
.B apply-settings
Require a completed matching system transaction, automatically capture
current settings, stage the result, and rename it into the user's home.
Only explicitly absent files are removed; excluded and unlisted paths
remain untouched. Upgrades retain edits and put changed defaults beside
them as .tamlinux-new files. A required user activation failure leaves the
transaction activating and returns exit 3; retry completes forward.
Retry verifies the exact saved change plan. Activation progress is bound
to the ordered user action plan; a changed profile restarts those actions
at zero. User actions must be safe to repeat.
.TP
.B session-ready
Require complete, agreeing system and user transactions. A settings-only
switch has its own id and also binds the exact completed system transaction.
A stale record cannot authorize a later product round trip.
This interface is the file-level barrier; actual login integration comes
with the tam nut and tam-deploy migration.
.TP
.BI settings " diff path"
Show the user file, its base default and its new default. Refuse secret
paths, detected secrets and binary settings rather than printing them.
.SS Product transactions (format 2, development interface)
.TP
.BI install " bundle"
Prepare a format 2 directory or portable
.B .tbundle
archive without replacing live files.
The bundle contains
.BR BUNDLE ,
.BR MANIFEST ,
.BR SETTINGS ,
.B tree/
and
.BR placed/ .
A trusted rootstock profile supplies permitted placement directories and
post-install actions.
.TP
.B apply
Apply a ready transaction before starting the desktop, or resume an
interrupted apply forward.
The transaction remains recorded through required post-install steps.
Apply renames files already written and synced by prepare.
Recovery refuses a destination whose contents match neither the old nor
the new manifest, leaving the transaction incomplete for console repair.
Without
.BR \-\-yes ,
old-file users leave the transaction ready.
With it, matching processes receive SIGTERM, followed by SIGKILL if they
remain after five seconds.
Processes match only complete paths in the selected installation's old
version tree or placed files, found through exe, arguments, mappings,
working directory or open descriptors.
On the live root, a running Hyprland blocks apply even with
.BR \-\-yes ;
the graphical-session guard never sends it a signal.
.TP
.B cancel
Disarm a staging or ready transaction.
An applying or activating transaction must finish forward.
.TP
.BI pack " dir " \-o " archive"
Verify a format 2 bundle and create its portable archive.
The archive begins with BUNDLE, MANIFEST and SETTINGS, followed by placed/
and tree/. Recipe material belongs under tree/recipe/ and is hashed with
the product tree.
.TP
.BI \-\-root " dir"
Select the format 2 installation root (default
.BR / ).
Product state is under
.BR var/lib/tamlinux ;
versions are under
.BR usr/lib/tamlinux/version .
An alternate root confines file placement and process matching to that
installation; an executable name alone never authorizes a signal.
.TP
.BI \-\-format " 1|2"
Select the seal format; the default remains format 1 for older recipes.
.TP
.BI \-\-target " text"
Add a target description to a format 2 BUNDLE.
Native ownership queries and target-runtime validation remain later work.
.TP
.B \-\-if-pending
With apply, do nothing when no transaction is pending.
An unfinished staging transaction is reported without applying it.
.SS Building
.TP
.BI build " recipes lock " \-o " dir " \-\-version " V"
Build a bundle from the exact commits in the lock file
.I lock
with the recipes in the directory
.IR recipes ,
and write it to
.IR dir ,
which must not exist.
Each locked commit is exported with
.BR "git archive" ;
then
.B make
and
.B "make install"
run with
.B PREFIX
set to
.IB slot /current
(from
.BR \-\-slot ,
which must be absolute) and
.B DESTDIR
set to a private directory.
Anything installed outside the prefix is refused, as are two components
installing the same path.
Each
.B make
runs in its own user and mount namespace at fixed paths under
.BR /tmp/tam\-bundle ,
with a fixed environment, so the same lock gives the same bundle whatever
the cache's path.
This needs unprivileged user namespaces; where they are turned off,
.B build
stops and says so.
Finished components are cached and reused when their commit, recipe, tools
and build dependencies are unchanged.
With
.B "index man"
in the bundle file,
.B build
also runs
.BR mandb (8)
on the bundle's
.B share/man
and seals the manual index with the pages; this needs man-db at build time.
Options:
.BR \-\-host ,
.BR \-\-repos ,
.BR \-\-built .
.TP
.BI seal " dir " \-\-name " N " \-\-version " V"
Write
.B BUNDLE
and
.B MANIFEST
for the built tree in
.IR dir .
This is
.BR build 's
last step, and how tests make bundles.
Options:
.BR \-\-host ,
.BR \-\-source ,
.BR \-\-tool ,
.BR \-\-needs ,
.BR \-\-after ,
.BR \-\-built .
.TP
.BI lock " component commit"
Set one component's commit in a lock file (default
.BR tamlinux.lock ,
or
.BR \-\-lock ).
Only that line is rewritten; comments and other lines stay as they were.
Adding a component that has no line yet needs
.BR \-\-url .
A revision shorter than a full commit is resolved only in a local clone
named with
.BR \-\-repos ,
never on a server.
.SS Checking
.TP
.BI verify " dir"
Hash every file in a bundle, kept tree or live tree and compare it with
.BR MANIFEST ,
then report extra, missing and edited entries.
.TP
.BI needs " dir"
Report unmet native requirements with the recorded install hints.
Format 1 retains the presence check for a command on
.B PATH
or a file. Format 2 first checks target architecture/CPU, libc loader and
rootstock profile. Its native package versions come from read-only Arch
pacman queries. Minimum versions are required unless the present file has
no native owner (reported as version unknown). Exact requirements hash
the file. QML module exports, isolated Python imports and non-executing
ELF dependency inspection provide the declared probes. Python imports
must be checked on the running rootstock, not an offline foreign root.
No install hint is executed.
.TP
.B status
Show the slot, its state directory, the live bundle, the previous bundle,
every kept tree with the reason it is protected, an interrupted switch if
there is one, the last log line, and the last result of session steps left
for each user.
The format-2 interface also reports runtime compatibility of every retained
product, including recovery versions. An unmet retained contract is a
failure; an outstanding transaction remains the stronger session barrier.
.TP
.B check
Compare the live tree with its
.BR MANIFEST .
A file whose size, inode, modification time and change time match the
values recorded after the switch is not hashed; with
.BR \-\-full ,
every file is hashed.
Edited, missing and extra files are listed.
.TP
.BI where " path"
Name the bundle, version and recorded hash of one file in the live tree.
.I path
is the file's full path, beginning with
.IB slot /current/\fR.
.SS Changing the slot
These commands take the slot's lock, then finish or undo an interrupted
switch first, as
.B recover
does, because an interrupted switch can leave kept trees misnamed and the
keep list stale.
With
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
they say what they would change and change nothing; if a switch was
interrupted, they say what recovery would do and exit with status 4.
.TP
.BI switch " bundle"
Install the bundle directory
.I bundle
and make it live.
The bundle is copied into a staging tree beside the live tree (unchanged
files are cloned from the live tree where the file system supports it, as
Btrfs does), synced to disk, and exchanged with the live tree in one
.BR rename (2)
call, so the slot never holds a half-installed tree.
The tree that was live moves to
.B kept/
and is recorded as previous.
.B switch
refuses a bundle built for another host, one whose native needs are
missing, and a live tree with edited, missing or extra files, which it
names; with
.BR \-\-save\-edits ,
it copies edited and extra files to the state directory's
.B saved/
and continues.
A bundle that is already kept is switched to without copying, as with
.BR back .
.TP
.BR back " [\fIbundle-id\fR]"
Make the previous kept tree, or the named one, live again.
The kept tree is checked against its
.B MANIFEST
first
.RB ( \-\-full
hashes every file) and refused if it differs.
Nothing is copied: the kept tree and the live tree are exchanged.
.TP
.B recover
Finish or undo an interrupted switch.
A switch stopped before the exchange is undone and the old tree stays
live; one stopped after it is finished.
A staging tree that never went live is removed.
If the live tree is neither side of the recorded switch,
.B recover
changes nothing, prints what it found and exits with status 4.
The other changing commands recover the same way before they start.
.B status
reports an interrupted switch and exits with status 4.
.BR check ,
.B where
and
.B after
read only the live tree, which the exchange keeps whole, so they still
answer, with a warning that names the interrupted switch.
.TP
.BI keep " bundle-id reason"
Protect a kept tree from
.BR prune .
.B previous
is reserved; it is set by
.B switch
and
.BR back .
.TP
.BI unkeep " bundle-id"
Stop protecting a kept tree.
.TP
.BR prune " [" \-\-yes ]
List kept trees that no reason protects, with their sizes, and remove them
only with
.BR \-\-yes .
On Btrfs, kept trees share unchanged data, so less space may come back
than the listed size.
.TP
.B after
Run the live bundle's post-install steps as the invoking user.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-slot " dir"
The slot (default
.BR /usr/lib/tamlinux ).
.B build
builds for this slot's live tree.
.TP
.BI \-\-state " dir"
The state directory (default: see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Say what would change; change nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-full
Hash every file instead of trusting unchanged file status.
.TP
.B \-\-save\-edits
Save edited and extra live files before switching.
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Let
.B prune
remove what it lists.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Print only errors.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " N\fR, " \-\-version " V"
The bundle's name and version, for
.BR seal ;
.B \-\-version
is also
.BR build 's
bundle version.
Alone,
.B \-\-version
prints
.BR tam-bundle 's
own version and the newest record format it reads.
.TP
.BI \-\-host " H"
Build the bundle for host
.I H
only (default
.BR any ).
.TP
.BI \-\-source " \(dqCOMPONENT COMMIT\(dq"
A source the bundle was built from; repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-tool " \(dqNAME VERSION\(dq"
A tool the bundle was built with; repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-needs " \(dqcommand|file TARGET [distro=package ...]\(dq"
A native command or file the bundle needs, with the packages that provide
it on each distribution; repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-after " STEP"
A post-install step; repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-built " TIME"
The build time recorded in
.BR BUNDLE .
It is not part of the hash.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIdir\fR"
Where
.B build
writes the bundle.
.TP
.BI \-\-repos " dir"
Take sources from local clones
.IB dir / component
before fetching from the lock file's URL.
.TP
.BI \-\-lock " file"
The lock file for
.B lock
(default
.BR tamlinux.lock ).
.TP
.BI \-\-url " url"
The repository URL for a component that
.B lock
adds.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of commands and options.
.SH PRIVILEGE
Run by a user other than root,
.BR switch ,
.BR back ,
.BR recover ,
.BR keep ,
.B unkeep
and
.B prune
on a slot that belongs to root (or that would be created under a directory
of root's) run again as root through
.BR /usr/bin/pkexec ,
with
.BR tam-bundle 's
own absolute path and the same arguments.
Relative paths given for the bundle and
.B \-\-state
are made absolute first.
The root run leaves the post-install steps that need the user's session to
the waiting user process, which runs them when
.B pkexec
returns.
The polkit action is
.BR org.tamlinux.bundle ;
.B "make install"
installs it under
.IB PREFIX /share/polkit\-1/actions/\fR,
and polkit reads actions only from
.BR /usr/share/polkit\-1/actions .
.PP
If authorization is refused or dismissed
.RB ( pkexec
exit status 126), or
.B pkexec
cannot run, as on a text console with no polkit agent (127),
.B tam-bundle
says so and nothing changes.
There, run the command with
.BR sudo (8),
then run
.B "tam-bundle after"
as yourself in the graphical session.
.PP
Dry runs, the reading commands, and every command on a slot of your own
need no privilege.
.SH POST-INSTALL STEPS
A bundle names its steps with
.B after
lines.
The list is fixed; a bundle cannot run anything else.
Each step is logged.
A failed step does not undo the switch, because the files are already in
place; rerun it with
.BR "tam-bundle after" .
.TP
.B user\-units\-reload
.BR "systemctl \-\-user daemon\-reload" .
.TP
.B qml\-cache\-purge
Run
.BR tam\-qmlcache\-purge ,
from the live tree's
.B bin/
or else from
.BR PATH ;
skipped if it is not installed.
.TP
.B shell\-restart
Run
.BR tam\-restart\-shell ,
found the same way.
.PP
All three need the user's session.
A root run started by
.B pkexec
leaves them to the user's waiting process; one started by
.B sudo
prints them as still to run.
Either way, the results are recorded in the state directory's
.BI session. uid
file, which
.B status
shows.
.SH FILES
.SS The slot
.TP
.IB slot /current/
The live tree, with its
.B BUNDLE
and
.BR MANIFEST .
.TP
.IB slot /kept/ bundle-id /
Kept trees.
.TP
.IB slot /staging. bundle-id /
Present only during a switch.
.SS State
For a slot that belongs to root, or whose nearest existing parent does,
state lives in
.BI /var/lib/tam\-bundle/ name /\fR;
for any other slot, in
.BI $XDG_STATE_HOME/tamlinux/tam\-bundle/ name /\fR
.RB ( ~/.local/state
when
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
is unset).
.I name
is the slot directory's last component.
.B \-\-state
names another directory.
Everything in it is readable by everyone; nothing in it is secret.
.TP
.B lock
Held for the length of a changing command.
.TP
.B switch
The intent file, present only while a switch or rollback is in progress.
.TP
.B log
One line per switch, rollback, keep, prune and post-install step.
.TP
.BI session. uid
Results of session steps that a root run left for that user.
.TP
.B keep
Protected bundle ids, each with its reason.
.TP
.BI stat/ bundle-id
File status of each live file, recorded after a switch, so
.B check
need not hash everything.
.TP
.BI saved/ bundle-id /
Files saved by
.BR \-\-save\-edits .
.SS Build cache
.TP
.B $XDG_CACHE_HOME/tamlinux/tam\-bundle/
.RB ( ~/.cache
when
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
is unset).
.B build/
holds finished components,
.B git/
bare mirrors fetched for locked commits, and
.B lock
allows one build at a time.
.SH FORMATS
Every record is plain UTF-8 text, one record per line, starting with a
.B format
line.
Paths are relative to the tree and come last on a line.
.B tam-bundle
reads every format up to its own and refuses a newer one, naming the
version it needs.
.TP
.B lock file
.B component
.I name url commit
lines, one per component, with the full commit.
.TP
.IB recipes / component .recipe
.BI component " name"\fR,
.B "build make"
(the only build kind),
.B output bundle
or
.B output build\-only
(a component other components build against, which contributes no files),
.BI make\-vars " VAR=VALUE ..."
(where
.BI @ component @
names a build-only component's prefix), and
.BI after\-build " component ..."\fR.
.B build\-needs
lists a native build package, optionally followed by >= and a minimum
version. Exact versions of these packages and the fixed toolchain base
are recorded in recipe/BUILDENV and hashed into the cache key and BUNDLE.
.B make\-targets
lists targets for the first make (default: the Makefile's default target);
.B install\-targets
lists targets for the second (default:
.BR install ).
Each list runs in one invocation with the same variables and prefix.
Targets must be names, never options or variable assignments.
A recipe may not set
.BR PREFIX ,
.B DESTDIR
or
.BR SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH .
PATH and SHELL are also reserved. Builds use PATH=/usr/bin and a new
network namespace. Sources are fetched before any build starts.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Fully hash cached build outputs before reuse. Ordinary cache checks use
the saved output manifest and unchanged file stats; damaged or mismatching
entries rebuild. This flag does not change the resulting bundle identity.
.TP
.IB recipes /bundle
The bundle-wide part of
.BR BUNDLE :
.BR name ,
.BR host ,
.B needs
and
.B after
lines, and
.B "index man"
to build the format-1 manual index. A format-2 recipe declares
.B target
records for the runtime contract and
.B place
records for allowed relative paths under usr/.
Components stage with PREFIX=/usr. Staged lib/tamlinux/current becomes
tree/ and the remaining allowed files become placed/usr/.
The recipe, component recipes, lock and BUILDENV are carried in tree/recipe/.
The installed rootstock profile independently checks placement.
.B file
.I packaging-path tree-path
adds a regular file from the packaging repository's HEAD commit as mode
0644. Paths must be relative. Format-2 destinations are SETTINGS, tree/
or placed/usr/. The destination may contain spaces.
Parents are created without following links. Existing files, links as
sources, and destinations BUNDLE and MANIFEST are refused.
When recipes are in Git, the bundle file, recipes and lock must match
HEAD in the same repository, and BUNDLE records
.BR "source packaging <commit>" .
Extra files come from that commit, regardless of working-copy edits.
Scratch recipes outside Git cannot use file entries.
.TP
.B BUNDLE
Identity:
.BR name ,
.BR version ,
.BR hash ,
.BR source ,
.BR tool ,
.BR host ,
.BR needs ,
.B after
and
.B built
lines.
.TP
.B MANIFEST
One line per entry, sorted by path:
.B d
.I mode
.B \-
.IR path ,
.B f
.I mode
.BI sha256: hex
.IR path ,
or
.B l
.B \-
.I target path
for a symbolic link, whose target must be relative and stay inside the
tree.
.PP
The bundle hash is SHA-256 over
.B BUNDLE
without its
.B hash
and
.B built
lines, followed by
.BR MANIFEST .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
State for slots not owned by root.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
The build cache.
.TP
.B PATH
Where native needs, build tools and post-install commands are found.
Each build sees only
.B PATH
from the caller's environment.
.TP
.BR PKEXEC_UID ", " SUDO_UID
In a root run, the user whose session steps are left to run.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Refused, or a check found problems.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
A switch completed, but a post-install step failed.
.TP
.B 4
An interrupted switch needs
.BR "tam-bundle recover" .
.SH EXAMPLES
Try a slot of your own, which needs no privilege.
Everything stays under
.BR /tmp/demo ;
remove that directory when done.
.PP
.RS
.nf
mkdir \-p /tmp/demo/v1/bin /tmp/demo/v2/bin
printf \(aq#!/bin/sh\enecho one\en\(aq > /tmp/demo/v1/bin/hello
printf \(aq#!/bin/sh\enecho two\en\(aq > /tmp/demo/v2/bin/hello
chmod 755 /tmp/demo/v1/bin/hello /tmp/demo/v2/bin/hello
tam\-bundle seal /tmp/demo/v1 \-\-name demo \-\-version 1
tam\-bundle seal /tmp/demo/v2 \-\-name demo \-\-version 2
alias tb=\(aqtam\-bundle \-\-slot /tmp/demo/slot \-\-state /tmp/demo/state\(aq
tb switch /tmp/demo/v1
tb switch /tmp/demo/v2
/tmp/demo/slot/current/bin/hello        # two
tb back
/tmp/demo/slot/current/bin/hello        # one
tb status
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build a bundle for the default slot from a lock file and local clones:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tam\-bundle build recipes tamlinux.lock \-o /var/tmp/tamlinux\-0.7.0 \e
    \-\-version 0.7.0 \-\-repos \(ti/src
tam\-bundle verify /var/tmp/tamlinux\-0.7.0
tam\-bundle switch /var/tmp/tamlinux\-0.7.0
.fi
.RE
.PP
Roll back, and find where a file came from:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tam\-bundle back
tam\-bundle where /usr/lib/tamlinux/current/bin/tam
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pkexec (1),
.BR sudo (8),
